---
permalink: /public/robots.txt
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#-
  robots.txt, written by the build rather than committed as a static file,
  because what it must say depends on which deploy this is.

  THE PERMALINK GOES THROUGH public/ ON PURPOSE, and /public/ is not part of
  the served path. The Vite plugin renames Eleventy's output to a temp folder
  and rebuilds with emptyOutDir, discarding anything it does not reference, and
  a plain robots.txt sitting in the output root is exactly that. Vite copies its
  publicDir verbatim to the output root, so this lands at /robots.txt. Same
  route _headers, _redirects and site.webmanifest take, and the same reason:
  passthrough alone silently lost all three. See DESIGN_SYSTEM.md, Deploy
  pipeline.

  Both halves of the indexing rule read deploy.indexable, so robots.txt and the
  meta tag in base.njk cannot disagree about the same deploy.

  ONE CAVEAT WORTH KNOWING. Disallow prevents crawling, which prevents a crawler
  ever seeing the noindex meta tag on those pages. That is the right shape for
  keeping a preview OUT of an index, and the wrong shape for getting one back
  out again: a URL already indexed needs to be crawlable so the noindex can be
  read. If a preview URL ever turns up in results, allow crawling temporarily
  and let the meta tag do the work.
-#}
# robots.txt for Circulation Studio
{% if deploy.indexable -%}
# Production deploy. Nothing is restricted.
User-agent: *
Allow: /
{%- else -%}
# NOT the production deploy ({{ deploy.branch or "local build" }}).
# This origin serves the same pages as the production host and must not compete
# with it in an index. Every page here also carries a noindex meta tag and a
# canonical pointing at production. See src/_data/deploy.js.
User-agent: *
Disallow: /
{%- endif %}
